      <title>City report: one 50-megawatt data center would draw as much electricity as 25,000 homes - supervisors weigh extending SF's data-center moratorium another 10.5 months</title>
      <link>https://focalnews.org/san-francisco-ca/local/new-sf-report-data-centers-could-strain-power-grid-compete-with-housing</link>
      <guid isPermaLink="true">https://focalnews.org/san-francisco-ca/local/new-sf-report-data-centers-could-strain-power-grid-compete-with-housing</guid>
      <pubDate>Wed, 11 Nov 2026 05:00:00 -0800</pubDate>
      <category>San Francisco</category>
      <description><![CDATA[<p><img src="https://jacobpedd.github.io/daily-digest/images/data-centers.png" alt="Generated illustration of a data center drawing power past San Francisco row houses" style="max-width:100%"/></p>
<p><b>SF's transmission capacity is about 1,000 MW against an 800 MW peak, and a new line could take a decade. The Lurie-commissioned report floats power caps, fees and 75-decibel generator limits but recommends nothing. Santa Clara, with 56 data centers, is weighing water restrictions.</b></p>
<p>Sources: <a href="https://focalnews.org/san-francisco-ca/local/new-sf-report-data-centers-could-strain-power-grid-compete-with-housing">San Francisco Focal</a></p>
